1. **Finding m**: We determine the smallest m such that m! is at least k. This m tells us how many elements at the end of the permutation will be rearranged.
2. **Generating Permutation**: Using the factorial number system, we generate the k-th permutation of the last m elements.
3. **Lucky Numbers**: We generate all lucky numbers up to n using a recursive approach, building numbers by appending 4 or 7.
4. **Counting Valid Positions**: We check each position from 1 to n. If the position is a lucky number and the element at that position (considering the permutation) is also a lucky number, we count it.

This approach efficiently handles large values of n by focusing only on the necessary part of the permutation and leveraging the properties of lucky numbers.

